<h2>OpenAI Astra — &ldquo;Ten Open Problems Solved&rdquo;, and This Time It Brought Certificates</h2>

<p>On August 1, 2026, OpenAI announced that an internal version of its next major model, <strong>Astra</strong>, had produced solutions to <strong>ten open problems that had seen no essential progress for a decade or more</strong>, spanning mathematics, quantum complexity and theoretical computer science. The headline: <strong>the first resolution of a 27-year-old question in group theory (the existence of a non-sofic group)</strong>. Nine months ago this same company was embarrassed within a day when a claim that &ldquo;GPT-5 solved ten Erdős problems&rdquo; fell apart — this time it arrived with <strong>249 pages of manuscripts and Lean certificates that a machine can check line by line</strong>. This article cross-verifies the official announcement against multiple press reports to lay out <strong>what was solved, why it counts as &ldquo;solved&rdquo;, and what mathematicians are saying</strong>.</p>

