<h3>Three-line summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li>An internal Astra produced new results on <strong>ten open problems</strong> — every one ships with a <strong>machine-checkable Lean certificate</strong>, and the combined token cost was <strong>about $2,000</strong> at Sol API rates.</li>
    <li>The case for &ldquo;solved&rdquo; has three layers — ① Lean <strong>machine-checks the logic of each proof line by line</strong> ② <strong>named mathematicians critically read the manuscripts</strong> (Sorin Popa for the Connes chapter) ③ and yet <strong>peer review has not happened</strong> — whether each formalization matches the original problem, and how much the results matter, is still on humans.</li>
    <li>The axis of the mathematical community's reaction is Terence Tao's phrase — from <strong>&ldquo;the era of proof scarcity into the era of proof abundance&rdquo;</strong> — with prestige shifting from <em>whoever proves it first</em> to <strong>whoever verifies and digests it</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Three terms before we start</h3>
