        <tr><td><strong>Lean<br>(proof assistant)</strong></td><td>A language that makes you <strong>write mathematical proofs like programs</strong>. Every step must be machine-readable, so if it compiles, you know <strong>&ldquo;this formal statement's proof has no logical gaps&rdquo;</strong> — without trusting whoever wrote it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Formalization</strong></td><td>Translating a human-language problem into <strong>a formal statement Lean can read</strong>. Lean only guarantees the formal statement — <strong>whether that statement is the same as the original problem</strong> still needs a mathematician's eyes. This is the central caveat of the announcement.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sofic group</strong></td><td>An infinite group is sofic if it can be &ldquo;approximated ever more precisely&rdquo; by finite permutations. Since Gromov introduced the concept in 1999, <strong>&ldquo;does a non-sofic group exist?&rdquo;</strong> went unanswered for 27 years — the headline result answers it (yes, with an explicit construction).</td></tr>
