<p>Spelled out: first, <strong>all ten results carry Lean certificates</strong> — the first time AI-produced mathematics at this scale has shipped with machine verification across the board, closing the classic &ldquo;is there a hole in the proof?&rdquo; doubt by machine. Second, the manuscripts' acknowledgements name <strong>real mathematicians as readers</strong> — the Connes rigidity chapter was read by <strong>Sorin Popa</strong>; the non-sofic chapter by <strong>Henry Bradford, Michael Chapman, Alon Dogon, Francesco Fournier-Facio and François Charles</strong>. They are <strong>critical readers, not co-authors</strong> — that distinction matters. Third, even so, <strong>none of the results has been peer-reviewed</strong>. Lean guarantees the truth of the formal statement and nothing more — <strong>whether that formalization is exactly the problem that was open, how novel it is against the literature, and how much it changes the field</strong> is what the mathematical community now has to establish.</p>

<h3>What the mathematicians say</h3>
<p><strong>Terence Tao</strong> (Fields Medal) has been repeating one frame all year, including right after this announcement — mathematics is moving <strong>&ldquo;from an era of proof scarcity into an era of proof abundance&rdquo;</strong>, and in that era <strong>&ldquo;some of the prestige previously awarded to being first to prove should transfer to those who verify and digest the proofs&rdquo;</strong>. He sketches a vision of <strong>&ldquo;big mathematics&rdquo;</strong> — large-scale division of labor between humans and machines — while also warning: a problem stamped &ldquo;solved&rdquo; that no human understands can <strong>kill off further interest in it</strong> (ICM 2026 talk · Mathstodon).</p>
<p><strong>Thomas Bloom</strong> (Manchester; curator of the Erdős problems catalogue) put it in two words: <strong>&ldquo;big news&rdquo;</strong>. The reason that carries weight is his track record — <strong>when an OpenAI executive claimed in October 2025 that GPT-5 had solved ten Erdős problems, Bloom was the one who publicly dismantled the claim</strong> (the &ldquo;solved&rdquo; problems already had answers in the literature; the model had mostly done good retrieval). The catalogue's own curator, who lived through that fiasco, calling this one &ldquo;big news&rdquo; is the most compressed testimony that the verification bar is different this time.</p>
<p>There is tension, too. The <strong>Leiden Declaration</strong> — published in June 2026 with the endorsement of the International Mathematical Union and signed by Tao among others — warns that AI companies <strong>bypassing peer review and announcing results by press release</strong> threatens the integrity of proof and attribution. OpenAI <strong>cited the declaration itself</strong> in this announcement — a gesture that it knows the bar and that Lean certificates plus named readers are its answer — but the announcement's form is still a corporate blog, not a journal, which is precisely the practice the declaration targets.</p>

<h3>What is Astra?</h3>
<p>The announcement drew as much attention for <strong>how</strong> it was made as for the results — OpenAI revealed its next major model's name not at a product event but <strong>inside a bundle of mathematics</strong> (hence the quip that it &ldquo;smuggled the Astra announcement into a blog post about math&rdquo;). Piecing the reports together: Astra is a model family designed for <strong>multiple agents collaborating on long-running tasks over hours or days</strong>; whether it becomes a new class alongside the existing <strong>Sol, Terra and Luna</strong> lines, or ships as GPT-6 or GPT-5.7, is <strong>undecided</strong> — even the name is tentative. It was demonstrated to policymakers in Washington and is reported to be the first model to go through the planned <strong>U.S. government pre-release review process</strong>. No release date, pricing or specifications have been announced.</p>

<h3>Why is this possible now? — the verification bottleneck moves</h3>
<p>The structural meaning of the announcement is the <strong>pairing of a generator (the LLM) with a verifier (Lean)</strong>. The chronic weakness of LLM mathematics was the &ldquo;plausible proof that leaks somewhere&rdquo;, and the human cost of finding the leak was the bottleneck. Forcing proofs through Lean makes <strong>the machine do the leak-finding for free</strong> — the model can discard failed attempts at will and search until something compiles, and the human job moves up a floor: from checking proofs to <strong>&ldquo;is the formalization right?&rdquo; and &ldquo;does this matter?&rdquo;</strong>. Tao's prestige shift is exactly the social translation of that structural change. The ~$2,000 total is symbolic for the same reason — for the first time, <strong>the marginal cost of generating a candidate answer to a 27-year-old problem has a price tag</strong>.</p>

